    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Registration dates now skip the five 2026 public holidays as well as weekends. The full test suite passes: 21 tests, 0 failures.\n\n**What changed**\n- **`util/BusinessDays.java`**: added the fixed holiday list (2026-01-01, 01-26, 04-25, 12-25, 12-28) and a public `isBusinessDay` check. `rollForward` now moves forward one day at a time until it reaches a day that is neither a weekend nor a holiday. Going one day at a time handles back-to-back cases: 2026-12-25 is a Friday holiday, the weekend follows, and Monday 12-28 is also a holiday, so the date lands on Tuesday 2026-12-29.\n- The controller still calls `BusinessDays.rollForward` for both create and update, so it needed no changes. The rule applies to dates the client sends and to the default (today's date).\n- **`BusinessDaysTests`**: added tests for:\n  - a holiday on a weekday\n  - a holiday on a weekend (2026-04-25 is a Saturday, so it rolls to the following Monday)\n  - the Christmas run of holiday, weekend, holiday\n  - the same month and day in another year (2027-01-26), which is not treated as a holiday\n- **`openapi.yml`**: updated the `registrationDate` description to mention the holiday rule.\n\n**Things to be aware of**\n- The holidays are exact 2026 dates, as the spec lists them. They don't repeat yearly, so from 2027 only weekends will be skipped until someone adds new dates.\n- As before, rolling forward can produce a date later than today, for example when a client supplies today's date and today is a holiday. The \"not later than today\" check runs on the supplied date before the roll, so this is accepted.\n- The acceptance test only checks that the default date isn't a Saturday or Sunday, so the holiday rule is covered only by the unit tests.\n- One line of the `openapi.yml` description wraps longer than the lines around it. Also, the file was changed on disk after my edit, so check it is in the state you expect.",
